<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · artifact</p><h1 class="enc-title">Priest-King</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 2000–1900 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/21st-century-bce">21st century BCE</a> · Also known as King-Priest</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Priest-King is a small steatite sculpture of a bearded man, found at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohenjo-daro">Mohenjo-daro</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sindh">Sindh</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>, in the 1920s and dated to about 2000–1900 BCE, in the city's Late Period. Broken off at the bottom and perhaps unfinished, it is 17.5 centimetres high, with a fillet round the head, an armband and a one-shouldered cloak patterned with trefoils that once held a red filling. Its name is a modern guess at the man's role. It is kept in the National Museum of Pakistan in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karachi">Karachi</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/priest-king.jpg" alt="Priest-King" width="320" height="413" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mamoon Mengal · CC BY-SA 1.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mohenjo-daro_Priesterk%C3%B6nig.jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
